// A mach-O-invalid `@section` specifier is a DIAGNOSTIC, not a process abort
// (DF-225f). Mach-O names a section by SEGMENT and section; LLVM does not
// report a bad one as an error a front end can catch — it calls
// `report_fatal_error`, so the compiler died with `LLVM ERROR: ... invalid
// section specifier ...` and exit -6, naming no declaration and no file.
//
// The ELF-shaped `.vector_table` here is exactly what a reader copies from the
// freestanding examples, which is what made this reachable by accident.
//
// The target is PINNED rather than inherited, so the test asserts the same
// thing on a Linux host as on a macOS one: the rule is a property of the object
// format, and an inherited triple would make this pass by not applying.
// EXPECT: error
// EXPECT-ERROR-CONTAINS: is not a valid section on this target
// EXPECT-ERROR-CONTAINS: __TEXT,vector_table
// EXPECT-ERROR-ABSENT: LLVM ERROR
// COMPILE-FLAGS: --target arm64-apple-darwin

@export
@section(".vector_table")
func kernel_entry() -> Int32 { 0 }

func main() {
    print("unreached")
}
